    "Model exhibits severe upward bias when recent history shows a sharp spike (e.g., >91) followed by immediate volatility; forecasts consistently overshoot the true mean reversion path.",
    "Forecasts fail to account for 'mean-reversion lag' where sentiment spikes (0.4-0.5) precede price drops, causing the model to predict higher prices than actual outcomes during high-volatility periods.",
    "When recent history contains a peak >91 followed by a drop below 80, the model's 30d forecast trajectory is systematically too flat and optimistic compared to the true outcome's oscillation.",
    "Sentiment history of 0.4-0.5 appearing after a price spike correlates with an immediate reversal in true outcomes, whereas the model continues its upward trend.",
    "Model underestimates volatility clustering; it predicts smoother paths while true outcomes show frequent swings of +/- 3-5 points within short windows."
  ]
